---
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  description={`How ${site.name} researches, builds, tests and updates its calculators and articles.`}
  path="/editorial-policy/"
  updated={site.legalUpdated}
  lede="Our standards for accuracy, sourcing, independence and corrections."
>
  <h2>Our standards</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} publishes calculators and explanations about money decisions that can have real consequences. We hold our work to
    four standards: it must be <strong>accurate</strong>, <strong>sourced</strong>, <strong>transparent</strong> and
    <strong>independent</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources we rely on</h2>
  <p>We build from primary sources wherever they exist:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Taxes:</strong> the Internal Revenue Code, IRS revenue procedures, publications and form instructions, and enacted legislation on Congress.gov.</li>
    <li><strong>Health and benefits:</strong> the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services, HealthCare.gov, the Department of Health and Human Services (poverty guidelines) and the Social Security Administration.</li>
    <li><strong>Lending and credit:</strong> the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (including Regulation Z), the Federal Reserve, Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac and HUD/FHA.</li>
    <li><strong>Small business:</strong> the U.S. Small Business Administration and IRS guidance on depreciation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where rules come from industry practice rather than law — such as typical HELOC loan-to-value limits or factoring fees — we
    describe them as typical ranges and say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every official figure our calculators use is listed, with a link to its source, on our
    <a href="/methodology/">methodology and data sources</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>How a calculator is built</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Research</strong> the rules, thresholds and formulas from primary sources.</li>
    <li><strong>Implement</strong> the math in a separate, testable module.</li>
    <li><strong>Test</strong> the module against known values: textbook amortization results, published tables (for example, checking that Medicare premiums match the statutory percentages) and hand-worked examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain</strong> the result on the page: how to use it, the formula, the assumptions and a worked example.</li>
    <li><strong>Review</strong> the page before publication and record the review date on the page.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Keeping figures current</h2>
  <p>
    Many figures change every year — tax brackets and standard deductions, Medicare premiums and IRMAA brackets, poverty guidelines
    and ACA contribution percentages, Social Security amounts. We track these release schedules and update affected calculators
    when agencies publish new numbers, usually in the fall for the following year. Each page shows its “Updated” date.
  </p>

  <h2>Use of software and AI tools</h2>
  <p>
    We use software tools — including automated testing and AI-assisted drafting — to help build and maintain the site. Every
    formula, figure and claim is checked against the sources listed on the page before it’s published, and responsibility for the
    content rests with our editorial team.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence from advertisers</h2>
  <p>
    The site is funded by advertising. Advertisers can’t see or influence our content before publication, and we don’t accept
    payment to feature, rank or recommend any lender, product or service.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you believe something is wrong, email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> with the page and the issue. We
    review every report, correct confirmed errors promptly, and update the page’s review date.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
